(project pricklypear-one-tree
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: one-tree — ONE BIG TREE as the storage substrate, typed nodes for data modelling")
 (status partial)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section one-tree

  (doc "One tree is the storage truth. Tables become projections. Nodes carry types.")
  (status partial)
  (implements image/lib/tree_store.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/crud.ml
              migrations/009_tree.sql scripts/verify_one_tree.sh)
  (verify
   (build "dune build @all")
   (test "image/test/tree_store_test.ml — 7 alcotest cases")
   (smoke "ksh scripts/verify_one_tree.sh (tree_verify + eval smoke on :18080)"))
  (details
   (doc (|
        =========================================================
        THE IDEA
        =========================================================
        Everything in Pricklypear is stored in ONE BIG TREE.
        The tree is the source of truth. Code, types, routes,
        data, docs — all of it lives as nodes in one rooted,
        typed, path-addressed tree. Nopales code IS tree
        content: a function's AST is a subtree.

        Today the image already simulates this tree on top of
        relational tables: branches/function_defs/type_defs/
        view_bindings reconstruct hierarchy via branch_id
        columns, parent pointers, and recursive-CTE override
        walks. Merge and promote are tree-diff algorithms
        written in SQL. The relational layer is scaffolding
        around what is already a tree. This chapter removes
        the scaffolding.

        This is the Smalltalk/GemStone lineage Pricklypear
        comes from: the persistent thing is the object graph,
        and queries are programs over the graph. One unix
        image process serializes structural mutation, so the
        classic one-tree-store objection (concurrent writers)
        mostly evaporates — one writer, and a "transaction"
        is: evaluate, commit the new root.

        WHAT CHANGES:
          - The tree is TRUTH. Rows are DERIVED.
          - Storage addressing is by PATH, not by
            (table, branch_id, name).
          - Merge/promote become honest three-way tree diffs
            instead of row-level branch inheritance.
        WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE:
          - The Nopales prim ABI. get/create/update/delete/
            rows/list-bindings/describe/deftype keep their
            signatures. The prim/effect seam — the core
            performs I/O via Control.perform_prim (OCaml 5
            effects; see runtime-effects-transition) and the
            rim handler answers through the Io.DB port — was
            built for exactly this swap. Nopales code and
            route handlers never see the difference. Lwt
            remains only as the v1 rim backend (Io_lwt); the
            direction is effects everywhere, Lwt is the
            replaceable power company, not the model.
          - Postgres remains the physical substrate (byte
            durability, backups, pg_dump). The tree is a
            Postgres-RESIDENT structure, not a new database.
          - additive-only migrations still govern whatever
            physical tables the tree store itself uses.
          - The effects direction (runtime-effects-transition):
            one-tree changes WHAT the rim reads/writes, not HOW
            the core talks to the rim. Core stays direct-style
            via Control.perform_prim; any Lwt still present is
            vestigial rim plumbing (Io_lwt), to be replaced
            (Eio or other) on the H3 schedule — it is NOT the
            model and should not grow.
   |))
  )
  (subsection tree-model
   (doc "The tree: one root, typed nodes, path addressing")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/tree_store.ml migrations/009_tree.sql)
   (details
    (doc (|
         ONE TREE, ONE ROOT:

           /
           ├── sys
           │   ├── pkg/<package>/<name>     function AST subtrees
           │   ├── type/<type>              type declarations
           │   └── route/<route>            route → handler bindings
           ├── user/<username>/...          same shape per user
           └── data
               └── <type>/<id>              typed data nodes
                                             (shared — see
                                             branching-interaction)

         BRANCHES ARE LENSES, NOT COPIES. A branch is a
         name → subtree-root pointer into the one tree
         (system/main, <user>/personal, <user>/dev are views).
         Fork = record a new pointer. Override = pin a subtree.
         Unpinned names resolve through the lens to main.
         This replaces today's "recursive CTE up the parent
         chain" with pointer chasing in the structure the data
         actually has.

         PHYSICAL FORM (v1, Postgres-resident):
           - Tree nodes serialized as JSONB (Nopales ASTs are
             already JSONB today — this formalizes it).
           - Path index: materialized path per node
             (ltree or text path column) so "give me all of
             /data/todo" and "give me subtree /sys/pkg/notes"
             are indexed lookups, not tree walks.
           - Per-subtree hashes (content-addressing later;
             detection + vault integrity now). A corrupt root
             must be detectable, and pp-sync snapshot must
             stay meaningful.

         ADDRESSES ARE STABLE. A node address
         (/user/alice/pkg/notes/doc/get, /data/flash-card/<id>)
         is the durable name other nodes reference. Moves go
         through an explicit refile op so references can be
         rewritten (see notes links-and-roam refile, and
         structural-editing for the shared tree-op protocol
         this plugs into).

         =========================================================
         PINNED (P0) — concrete v1 decisions
         =========================================================
         The tree is realized over TODAY's tables first; each
         existing row maps to exactly ONE typed node. Paths are
         DERIVED from row identity (no behavior change):

           PATH GRAMMAR (branch-aware code/types/routes):
             fn     b.<branch_id>.fn.<name>
             type   b.<branch_id>.type.<name>
             route  b.<branch_id>.route.<route>
           DATA (shared, branch-free — branching.data kept):
             datum  data.<type_name>.<id>
           META (system, branch-free):
             user/cred/session/media  meta.<kind>.<id>

           Branch inheritance is NOT baked into the path. The
           override-walk (recursive CTE up branches.parent_id,
           depth ASC, first-occurrence dedup) is REPLACED at
           read time by: try path under self, then parent, up
           the chain — first hit wins. Rehydrate loads
           ancestors-first (depth DESC) so child pins override
           by re-define. Same semantics, honest representation.

           NODE ENVELOPE (jsonb payload per node):
             { v: 1,
               kind: fn|type|route|datum|user|cred|session|media,
               path: text (materialized),
               type: text (datum: type_name; else node class),
               body: json (fn AST / type fields / route
                     {handler,visibility} / datum fields),
               author: text,
               doc: text|null (fn only),
               hash: hex (sha256 over canonical body),
               created_at, updated_at }
           Hash covers ONLY canonical body (not path/author) so
           content-corruption is detectable independent of
           placement. Per-subtree root hashes compose from leaf
           hashes (content-addressing later).

           PHYSICAL LAYOUT (migration 009_tree.sql; additive):
             tree_nodes(
               id uuid PK DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
               path ltree NOT NULL UNIQUE,
               kind text NOT NULL,
               type_name text,                 -- datum only
               payload jsonb NOT NULL,
               hash text NOT NULL,
               author text NOT NULL,
               created_at timestamptz DEFAULT now(),
               updated_at timestamptz DEFAULT now())
             -- GIN on payload; btree (kind,type_name);
             -- ltree GiST on path for subtree queries.
             tree_branches(                    -- lens registry
               branch_id uuid PK REFERENCES branches(id),
               root_path ltree NOT NULL,       -- b.<branch_id>
               parent_id uuid,                 -- kept in v1
               head_hash text)                 -- subtree root hash
           branches table stays in v1 (branch_id uuid + parent
           chain); tree_branches adds lens pointer + head hash.
           /data and /meta are NOT under any b.<id> — shared
           subtrees every lens can see.

           LENS = branch_id + its parent chain. A read resolves
           a logical name by walking the chain's path prefixes.
           This is the v1 lens; true content-addressed pointer
           moves (fork = new pointer, promote = move main head)
           arrive in P5 once the tree is truth.

         NEXT FREE MIGRATION: 009 (001..008 exist). tree_store
         module: image/lib/tree_store.ml.

         SINGLE WRITER: the one image process serializes
         structural mutation. A commit is "evaluate, publish
         new root." No lock manager, no merge-on-write races
         inside a commit. Concurrency control moves up to
         with-transaction over tree subtrees.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "addressing: resolve /data/todo/<id> and /sys/pkg/notes/<fn> to nodes via path index")
    (smoke "subtree hash detects corruption of a stored node")))
  (subsection typed-nodes
   (doc "Every node has a type; types are the data-modelling layer")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/tree_store.ml image/lib/crud.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Every node in the tree carries a TYPE. This realizes
         object-model.type-system (planned) as the tree's
         data-modelling layer — types are how the tree is
         structured, not an annotation on rows.

         KINDS OF TYPED NODE:
           - CODE nodes — type is the AST shape (defn, deftype,
             route). A function IS a subtree whose root is a
             `defn` node.
           - DATA nodes — a product type, declared in Nopales:
               (deftype note        (fields (title :string)
                                     (body :string) (tags :array :string)))
               (deftype flash-card  (fields (front :string)
                                     (back :string) (due :timestamptz)
                                     (deck :ref flash-deck)))
               (deftype flash-deck  (fields (title :string) ...))
             Notes, flash cards, todos, calendar events,
             workouts — every product's data model is a set of
             typed node shapes under /data/<type>/.
           - SCHEMA nodes — the type declarations themselves
             live in the tree under /sys/type/, so a type is
             inspectable data like everything else (the
             Smalltalk metamodel-browser feeling).

         FIELD TYPES: as object-model.type-system —
           :string :int :bool :timestamptz :json
           :ref <type>      → a PATH reference to a typed node
           :array <type>    → homogeneous
         In one-tree, a :ref is literally a tree path, not an
         opaque id — dereferencing is a path lookup, and the
         tree can answer "what points at this node" (backlinks)
         from the path index. This is the query roam wants.

         VALIDATION AT THE BOUNDARY: the prim layer checks a
         node's fields against its type declaration on
         create/update. Nopales stays dynamically typed
         internally (per roerick: the product surface tolerates
         jank); the tree boundary is where shapes are enforced.
         The OCaml outer shell is where correctness is proven
         (formal-verification).

         NO INHERITANCE, NO SUBTYPING — composition via :ref,
         exactly as object-model.type-system already rules and
         storage.no-inheritance already argues. One-tree does
         not re-open that decision; typed nodes are flat shapes
         composed by reference.

         RELATION TO TODAY: data_rows rows ARE these typed data
         nodes, packed as jsonb; type_defs rows ARE the schema
         nodes. One-tree does not invent new data semantics —
         it gives the existing semantics their natural shape
         and makes types load-bearing (validation, backlinks,
         structural ops) instead of documentary.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "create a flash-card node missing a required field → clean rejection naming the field")
    (smoke ":ref deref resolves via path; backlink query lists nodes referencing a given node")))
  (subsection projections
   (doc "SQL over the tree: relational rows are derived, not truth")
   (status implemented)
   (implements migrations/009_tree.sql)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The tree is truth, but ad-hoc relational query is how
         you SEE it. Postgres stored procedures / views /
         materialized projections DERIVE (type_name, id,
         fields) rows from the tree:

           - /data browsing, /types, admin grep-queries
           - reporting, pp-sync export, debugging
           - the existing CRUD prim RESULT SHAPES (rows returns
             (id . fields) pairs) are served from a projection
             even once the store is a tree

         DEPENDENCY DIRECTION FLIPS. Today rows are truth and
         the tree is reconstructed by CTE; after one-tree the
         tree is truth and rows are a cache. Projections are
         REBUILDABLE — drop and regenerate from the tree at
         any time. Nothing authoritative lives only in a
         projection.

         OPTION, NOT GATE: the stored-procedure projection
         layer is how relational tooling keeps working during
         and after the cutover. Nopales product code keeps
         calling the same prims; only the prim layer's backing
         moves from tables to tree + projection.

         INDEXING: projections and the path index are what make
         "all nodes of type X" and "subtree at P" fast. A full
         tree walk is never the query path for product or admin
         surfaces.

         OUT OF SCOPE (v1): exposing raw SQL to Nopales
         product code. The projection layer serves the image
         and admin tooling; the Nopales surface stays prims.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "rows 'todo' returns the same (id . fields) pairs backed by a projection over the tree")
    (smoke "rebuild projection from tree; diff against pre-rebuild state: identical")))
  (subsection branching-interaction
   (doc "Branches as tree lenses; the /data question; merge as tree-diff")
   (status partial)
   (implements image/lib/store.ml image/lib/tree_store.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         BRANCH = POINTER INTO THE ONE TREE. Fork records a
         new lens; override pins a subtree; promote moves main's
         pointer to the lens's subtree. This is a strict
         simplification of today's parent_id + branch_id-row
         model: the semantics are IDENTICAL, the representation
         is honest.

         MERGE / PROMOTE = THREE-WAY TREE DIFF. With ASTs as
         subtrees, merge diffing is structural (per-node), and
         last_synced becomes a root-hash comparison. This
         realizes branching.ast-nodes (planned) and feeds
         branching.change-set and review-merge: a change set IS
         a tree diff.

         THE /data DECISION (the one real semantic change).
         Today: data_rows has NO branch_id — shared data
         (branching.data). One-tree offers two placements:
           A. SHARED SUBTREE — /data/<type>/<id> lives outside
              any branch, lenses reference it. Closest to
              today's semantics; data never versioned.
           B. VERSIONED DATA — /data lives inside each lens,
              fork versions data too.
         DEFAULT: A (shared /data subtree), preserving
         branching.data exactly. B is deferred and NOT v1 —
         branching.borg's "data is never versioned by branch
         and never wiped by deploy" rule is load-bearing
         product behavior.

         TYPE_DECLS: today per-branch rows, table shape shared
         (branching.schema-authority). Under one-tree, schema
         nodes at /sys/type/ follow CODE branching rules
         (branchable, promoted), while /data instances stay
         shared. Branch code reading shared data against a
         newer/older type decl follows the existing
         additive-only tolerance rule.

         COTENANT ISOLATION: lenses enforce
         multi-user.cotenant-isolation — a session's lens
         confines reads/writes to its visible subtree plus
         shared /data, same guarantee as today's
         session+branch scoping, expressed as path confinement.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "fork = new lens; override on lens A invisible to lens B; promote moves main pointer")
    (smoke "shared /data: a todo created on alice/personal is visible from bob/dev (today's rule)")))
  (subsection migration-plan
   (doc "Phased cutover: shadow tree → flip reads → projections derive → retire tables")
   (status partial)
   (implements migrations/009_tree.sql image/lib/store.ml image/lib/crud.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         NO BIG BANG. The prim ABI is stable throughout; each
         phase is reversible.

         PHASE 0 — ADDRESSING SCHEME (pure design; this chapter).
           Freeze the path grammar (/sys/…, /user/…, /data/…),
           node envelope (type tag + payload + hash), and the
           lens model. No code.

         PHASE 1 — SHADOW TREE.
           A writer mirrors function_defs/type_defs/
           view_bindings/data_rows into the one tree on every
           commit. Tables remain truth; the tree is a derived
           copy. Proves addressing, path indexing, hashing —
           fully reversible.

         PHASE 2 — FLIP READS.
           The rim's store backend (Io.DB impl; Io_lwt today)
           serves reads from the tree behind the unchanged
           prim ABI; tables become the write-through fallback.
           Diff tree-read vs table-read in CI for a soak
           period.

         PHASE 3 — TREE IS TRUTH, ROWS ARE PROJECTIONS.
           Writes commit to the tree; the relational shape
           becomes a stored-procedure/view projection for /data,
           /types, admin. Retire the scaffolding tables (via
           additive-only migration leaving them inert, per
           house rule — never DROP).

         MIGRATIONS AS TREE TRANSFORMS. Schema change stops
         being DDL and becomes Nopales-level migration
         FUNCTIONS over subtrees (more in-spirit; real work).
         Physical tables the tree store uses still change only
         via additive-only SQL migrations.

         BLAST RADIUS: per-subtree hashes + pp-sync snapshot as
         the vault keep corruption detectable and recoverable;
         a corrupt subtree is rebuildable from snapshot.

         DEPENDS ON / FEEDS:
           - object-model.type-system (planned) — realized here
             as typed-nodes.
           - branching.ast-nodes, change-set, review-merge —
             tree diff is their substrate.
           - structural-editing — one shared tree-op protocol
             over outlines AND the storage tree.
           - notes links-and-roam — backlinks come from :ref
             path references.
           - migrations.additive-only — physical-shape rule
             unchanged.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "phase 1: every commit appears in shadow tree within the same transaction")
    (smoke "phase 2 soak: zero diffs between tree-read and table-read over CI window")
    (smoke "phase 3: drop-write to scaffolding tables; /data + /types served from projection")))
  (subsection non-goals
   (doc "What one-tree is NOT")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         - NOT a new database. Postgres stays the physical
           substrate; the tree is Postgres-resident JSONB.
         - NOT versioned user data in v1. /data stays shared
           (branching.data preserved); per-branch data
           versioning is explicitly deferred.
         - NOT a type system for Nopales. Typing lives at the
           tree/storage boundary; the language stays dynamic
           (roerick: product surface tolerates jank; the OCaml
           shell is where proofs live).
         - NOT re-opening inheritance. No INHERITS, no
           subtyping (storage.no-inheritance, object-model
           .type-system stand).
         - NOT a REST/GraphQL surface. The image remains the
           API via prims + eval.
         - NOT raw SQL in product code. Projections serve the
           image and admin tooling only.
   |))
   )
  )
 )
)
